<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Academy of Geneva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> in 1559 to train Reformed pastors and teachers, with Theodore Beza as its first rector. It drew students from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and elsewhere and spread Calvinist teaching across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It developed into the University of Geneva in 1873.</p></div>
